        "original_id": "a217c730-654f-5ced-8b5b-c8702368193b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: E. B. Babcock\n- Plants: Crepis, Isatis\n- Concepts: Asteraceae, Brassicaceae, polyploidy, apomixis\n\nFacts:\n- Crepis is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants in the family Asteraceae.\n- Crepis is commonly called hawksbeard or hawk's-beard in some regions, but it is distinct from the related genus Hieracium, which has a similar common name.\n- Crepis superficially resembles the dandelion and usually has branching scapes with multiple flower heads, although solitary heads can also occur.\n- Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae.\n- Isatis is native from the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.\n- E. B. Babcock is particularly known for using field investigations and extensive experiments to study the entire polyploid apomictic genus Crepis.\n- E. B. Babcock recognized 196 species within Crepis.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
